<p>I'm always willing to reevaluate after an extensive rewrite. However, it's going to take a major reworking to address the points above, and even then you've got a challenge ahead of you if your aim is to make &quot;how far the Foundation is willing to go&quot; the main theme of the piece, for numerous reasons. You'll want to weigh the issues of one particular user against those structural obstacles.</p>
<p>The geopolitics aren't my main issue really. In any event, I just don't see how it would work; moving the date five years isn't going to make a difference, especially in the case of relations between Russia and Japan (being <em>closer</em> to the Russo-Japanese War isn't going to improve matters any). And that doesn't speak to the main problems, which are A) four nations that each view themselves as Great Powers in the traditional 19th century sense are unlikely to cooperate that closely, and B) each of the four has vastly differing cultural norms, meaning their views of consensus reality are likely to be vastly different, assuming of course that all of them <em>want</em> to isolate such things.</p>

